          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"The expression directly presents Pierre's love for Hélène as true. Its hesitant and formulaic delivery may affect sincerity, but not its clear truth-conditional force as a love avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"«Теперь уж поздно, всё кончено; да и я люблю ее», подумал Пьер.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.82,\"reason\":\"In the represented situation, Pierre weakly enacts or ratifies his socially assigned role as Hélène's fiancé by uttering the expected formula. The expression itself, however, does not explicitly promise marriage or state any concrete obligation, so the surrounding engagement should not be transferred wholesale onto it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Пьер молча держал руку своей невесты\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The words are not represented as a reflex-like affective outburst. Pierre pauses, searches for the conventionally appropriate phrase, and then deliberately says it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"«Что-то такое особенное говорят в этих случаях», думал он, но никак не мог вспомнить, чтò такое именно говорят в этих случаях.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"сказал он, вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression moderately functions as performance of an expected courtship or betrothal script. The narration foregrounds Pierre's recollection that this is what one says on such occasions and his embarrassment at its inadequate sound.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"слова эти прозвучали так бедно, что ему стало стыдно за себя\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Pierre and Hélène have been treated by those present as an engaged couple and are then left alone. After Hélène kisses him, Pierre recalls the phrase conventionally expected in such a situation and tells her, formally, “I love you.”\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The target only weakly ratifies Pierre's role as Hélène's fiancé; it contains no explicit promise to marry or clearly specified future obligation. Hélène gives no recorded verbal response after the expression. Their marriage a month and a half later shows the broader relationship proceeded, but does not by itself establish immediate uptake of this particular avowal as a promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as Leo Tolstoy's War and Peace, depicting Pierre Bezukhov and Hélène Kuragina. The assessment uses only the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supplies Pierre's attraction, unease, internal claim of love, and formulaic motivation, but it does not conclusively settle the sincerity or precise emotional character of his love. The target alone does not specify the terms of any relational undertaking.\"}"
        }
